  "title": "Billing Module Payment Change",
  "updatedAt": 1794016030756,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "oVkAkwBwvXokap9I",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "What was the most recent significant change to the billing module's bill payment handling, and why was it made?",
      "generation": "g-ab363e",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The most recent significant change to bill payment handling in `openmrs-module-billing` is PR **#155, \"Payments should be associated with a cashier\"** (commit `b52412a0a7925469183c2590937d6720228f9ec6`, 2026-04-01). It added a mandatory `cashier` (an OpenMRS `Provider`) to every new payment, so each payment row now records who took the money. It was made to fix the gap that a payment previously had no notion of who processed it — and notably it is the *second* attempt: the first, PR #109 / O3-5197 (\"Each payment should have an associated Cashier\", commit `edd63790481fe565d8dbeb767a1ac8f29a1fb636`), was reverted in PR #147 (commit `7dfc3af97f42e94050d309742e38b580d3d8870e`) and then re-landed in #155.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**What changed**",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "1. **Model** — `Payment` gained a `cashier` field of type `org.openmrs.Provider` [1], alongside the existing `amount` and `amountTendered`.\n2. **Persistence** — a `provider_id` column was added to the payment table with an FK to `provider` [2].\n3. **Server-side defaulting** — when a bill is saved through REST, any new payment with no cashier inherits the authenticated user's provider record. `setBillPayments` does this once per save and throws a `RestClientException` if the logged-in user has no provider [3], delegating to `getCurrentCashier` / `assignCurrentCashier` [4].\n4. **Client override** — a client may still send an explicit cashier, accepted as a provider UUID by the `@PropertySetter(\"cashier\")` in `PaymentResource` [5], and it is exposed in the default/full representation [6].\n5. **Validation** — `BillValidator.validateNewPaymentsHaveCashier` rejects the save with `billing.error.paymentCashierRequired` if a new, non-voided payment still has no cashier [7]. The check is deliberately narrowed (see the comment at [8]) to payments where `getId() == null` and `voided == false`, so **legacy payments written before the column existed stay valid**.",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Why the ordering inside the validator matters**",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`validateNewPaymentsHaveCashier` is called before `validateLineItemsNotModified` [9]. That order is the whole point of the trap the PR had to fix: the line-item check runs a DB query, which makes Hibernate auto-flush the transient payments, assign them IDs, and thereby defeat the `payment.getId() == null` test — so the cashier requirement would silently stop firing. If you reorder those two lines, the validation regresses.",
          "citations": [
            9
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: \"Bill payment: cashier assignment\"\n---\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant UI as Billing UI\n    participant BR as BillResource\n    participant BV as BillValidator\n    participant DB as Database\n    UI->>BR: POST /billing/bill with payments\n    BR->>BR: setBillPayments, default cashier from current provider\n    BR->>BV: saveBill triggers validation\n    BV->>BV: validateNewPaymentsHaveCashier first\n    BV->>BV: then validateLineItemsNotModified, may auto-flush\n    BV-->>BR: reject if new payment has no cashier\n    BR->>DB: persist bill and payments with provider_id\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "On the client side, `openmrs-esm-billing-app` already expects this shape: `Payment` carries a `cashier: Provider` [10], and the payload type sends the cashier UUID as a string [11], so callers can either supply it or let the backend fill it in from the session. The same frontend also has a configured default cashier provider UUID [12].",
          "citations": [
            10,
            11,
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "One caveat on sourcing: pull requests for `openmrs-module-billing` were never ingested into my index, so I could not read the PR bodies or review comments for #109, #147 or #155 — the \"why\" above comes from the commit messages and bodies, the reverted/re-landed commit pairs, and the code itself, not from the ticket text. The O3-5197 key appears only in commit `edd6379`'s subject; I did not read the ticket.\n</answer>",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
